rue] [17592186251845 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533798 true] [17592186251845 1111 #uuid "50bbc2d4-d324-5156-af31-2ed249286017" 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1017 #edb/ref 17592186251847 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1089 "q-117803" 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1100 "What is the value of $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2$ at its local maximum points?" 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1113 #uuid "854cbf28-abb4-5877-a950-3499f7238e55" 13194139533798 true] [17592186251846 1145 "On a positive branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$ are **local minimums** of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$  For now, we're interested in the local minimums (not local maximums) of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ because we see that $f(x)$ contains a vertical reflection.\n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $-3$ and adding $2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\ge 1 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\le -3 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -3+2 \\\\  & -3\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})+2\\le -1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-1$ at its local maximum points." 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251848 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251849 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251850 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251851 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1000 #edb/ref 17592186251852 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533798 true] [17592186251847 1041 "selection" 13194139533798 true] [1